{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem appeared to him.</b> One needs to know why Scripture changed and placed the one who sees before the One who is seen, beginning to speak about him [the seer] first — for it should have said ״וַיֵּרָא ה׳ אֵלָיו״. And so we find that in all the preceding places He spoke thusly (Genesis 12:7): ״וַיֵּרָא ה׳ אֶל אַבְרָם״ וְגוֹ״ לְזַרְעֲךָ״, and ״וַיֵּרָא ה׳ אֶל״ וְגוֹ״ אֲנִי אֵל שַׁדַּי״ (Genesis 17:1)." "<b>Further,</b> one needs to know what was said to Abraham in this prophecy. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 86b) that He came to visit him because of the pain of his circumcision, except that no allusion to this matter is mentioned in Scripture." "<b>And it appears</b> that the intention of Scripture is to inform that HaShem caused His Shekhinah to rest upon him, and he became a chariot for the Shekhinah. And you will find that they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 47:6) that the Patriarchs are a chariot for the Shekhinah. And this is its saying <b>and [HaShem] appeared to him</b> — that HaShem revealed His Shekhinah to him. And for this reason it preceded the word ״אֵלָיו״ to the mention of HaShem, to say that the Shekhinah was revealed upon him, which would not have been conveyed by saying ״וַיֵּרָא ה׳ אֵלָיו״, for [in that order] HaShem would interrupt between the revelation and the one upon whom it is revealed. And understand this. And for this reason you will find that ״וַיֵּרָא״ is never again said in all the prophetic utterances spoken to Abraham after this, but only ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳״, for [the Shekhinah] is already present before him, a crown upon his head." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that after Abraham circumcised himself, there was then revealed in his flesh the holy seal of the yod (Tanhuma Tzav), and this is its saying <b>and [HaShem] appeared to him</b>. And this is the statement of the Zohar (Zohar 1:95), and this is its language: ״וכל מאן דשריא ביה רשימא קדישא שריא ביה שכינתא״ — and anyone upon whom the holy seal rests, the Shekhinah rests upon him." "<b>Further,</b> it intends [to say] that in the aftermath of the circumcision, HaShem appeared to him in the completeness of the totality of the supernal light, for there are in the aspect of prophetic vision, blessed be He, gradations — one higher than the other — and what he attained after the circumcision was complete prophecy. And this the Scripture informed us by saying <b>and [HaShem] appeared to him</b>, and it did not say ״וַיֵּרָא ה׳ אֵלָיו״, for it intended to say that what reached him from the vision was every aspect of the Name הוי״ה, the honored Name, and for this reason it preceded ״אֵלָיו״ and only afterward mentioned what is grasped, which would not have been conveyed if it had preceded to mention the Name before mentioning the word ״אֵלָיו״. And examine this carefully." "It further intends to say <b>וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו</b> — meaning: for Abraham's own need Elohim came, and here there is an allusion to what our Sages, of blessed memory, said concerning visiting the sick. And this is the language of our Sages in tractate Bava Metzi'a (86b): Rabbi Ḥama bar Ḥanina said: it was the third day from his circumcision and HaShem came and visited him — thus far is their language. And one needs to know his reason: who told him that it was the third day? And if from what the verse says (Genesis 34:25): ״בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁלִישִׁי בִּהְיוֹתָם״ etc. — but surely from the words of our Sages in tractate Shabbat (134a) it explicitly implies that the first and second day are more dangerous than the third day. And although they distinguished there between an adult and a minor, that an adult's flesh does not heat up as much — this is specifically regarding the third day; but the first and second day, for everyone, are more dangerous than the third day." "<b>And do not</b> find it difficult concerning the sons of Jacob, that they did not come upon the wounded on the first and second day — for there they did not pay attention to the time that is more dangerous, but rather to the time when they are weaker so that they would not stand against them; and by that measure, on the third day they were weaker than on the first and second day. But regarding the matter of visiting the sick, since the first and second days are in the grip of greater danger than the third day, and are at the very least equivalent to it — who could say that the Holy One, blessed be He, did not come on the first days? And I saw in the glosses of the Tur (Tur, Yoreh De'ah 335, 5) that he did not write 'the third day,' but rather 'it teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, came to visit the sick person'; but in the Talmud before us we have the reading 'the third day,' etc." "<b>And behold,</b> according to what the Ran wrote — that the first day and the third day are dangerous, but the second day he explained that one does not desecrate the Sabbath for it — there is a reason in the matter. Since the second day involves no concern of danger, HaShem judged it during the first three hours of the day, when people do not visit the sick; and the first day too they did not visit because of what they said in tractate Nedarim (40a), and this is its language: Rava said: on the first day of illness one should say to them: do not reveal it to anyone, so as not to worsen his fortune, and Rashi explained: so that they not talk about him — thus far. And this is the reason that Rav Ḥama did not say that HaShem visited him on the first day, so that people not talk, etc.; and moreover, when a great king comes, the matter becomes publicly known throughout all the worlds. And although they said in the Jerusalem Talmud of tractate Pe'ah (chapter 3) that relatives enter immediately to visit him, and the ruling of the Maharik, siman 335 — see his words there — and the reason is that for relatives the matter does not become publicly known, and so the later authorities wrote — one should not raise the difficulty that HaShem our Elohim is very close to us, for in any case there is publicity and the matter becomes publicly known. But according to the opinion that there is no difference between the first and second day and all are more dangerous than the third day, one needs to know why he said 'the third day'; and perhaps those who hold this view read according to the reading of the Tur."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:09.225979585Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}